[{"location":"index.html#On-the-Problem-of-Minimizing-the-Epidemic-Final-Size-for-SIR-Model-via-Social-Distancing","page":"Introduction","title":"🧪 On the Problem of Minimizing the Epidemic Final Size for SIR Model via Social Distancing","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#Introduction","page":"Introduction","title":"📘 Introduction","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#**Context-and-Motivation**","page":"Introduction","title":"Context & Motivation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"We revisit the problem of minimizing the epidemic final size in the SIR model through social distancing interventions.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Traditionally, this problem assumes a fixed interval structure for the timing of interventions. In contrast, we investigate a more flexible approach: $L^1$-constrained controls that limit total intervention effort, rather than when and how it's applied.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Key insight:","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Even with this generalization, the optimal control still occurs over a single time interval—indicating no gain from fragmenting interventions across multiple periods.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"However, if the infection rate $\\beta(t)$ changes over time, then splitting interventions into two disjoint periods may become optimal.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#**Our-Contributions**","page":"Introduction","title":"Our Contributions","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#Theoretical-Results","page":"Introduction","title":"🔍 Theoretical Results","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Constant Transmission Rate\nOptimal control is supported on one continuous interval. Concentrated efforts outperform spread-out strategies.\nPiecewise Constant Transmission Rate\nWhen $\\beta(t)$ changes once (e.g., from $\\beta_1$ to $\\beta_2$), the optimal policy may involve at most two separate interventions.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#Numerical-Simulations","page":"Introduction","title":"💻 Numerical Simulations","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"We support our theoretical claims with simulations, covering:","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Constant $\\beta$\nSingle-change $\\beta$ (piecewise constant)","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"These validate the analytical results and quantify the differences between intervention strategies.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#The-SIR-Model-with-Control","page":"Introduction","title":"⚙️ The SIR Model with Control","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#**System-Dynamics**","page":"Introduction","title":"System Dynamics","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"We use the classical SIR model with a control variable $u(t)$ (representing social distancing):","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"begincases\ndotS(t) = -(1-u(t))beta(t) S(t)I(t) \ndotI(t) = (1-u(t))beta(t) S(t)I(t) - gamma I(t)\nendcases","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"$S(t)$, $I(t)$: Susceptible and infected population fractions\n$u(t) \\in [0, \\bar{u}]$: Intervention intensity\n$\\beta(t)$: Transmission rate\n$\\gamma$: Recovery rate","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"The control $u(t)$ modifies the infection term, reducing contacts based on intervention intensity.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#**Key-Parameters**","page":"Introduction","title":"Key Parameters","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Transmission Rate: $\\beta(t) > 0$ (time-varying)\nRecovery Rate: $\\gamma > 0$\nReproduction Number: $R_0(t) = \\beta(t)/\\gamma$","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#**Transmission-Scenarios**","page":"Introduction","title":"Transmission Scenarios","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"We analyze two transmission models:","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Constant $\\beta$","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"   beta(t) = beta_0 quad forall t geq 0","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Piecewise Constant $\\beta$","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"   beta(t) = begincases\n   beta_1  textif  0 leq t  T_c \n   beta_2  textif  t geq T_c\n   endcases","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"where $T_c$ is the time of the transmission change.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#Optimal-Control-Formulation","page":"Introduction","title":"🎯 Optimal Control Formulation","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html#**Objective**","page":"Introduction","title":"Objective","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Our goal: Minimize the final epidemic size = maximize $S(\\infty)$.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"J(u) = S(infty) = lim_t to infty S(t)","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"This represents preserving the largest possible fraction of the susceptible population.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#**Constraints**","page":"Introduction","title":"Constraints","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"We impose an $L^1$ budget constraint on the intervention:","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"u(cdot)_L^1 = int_0^infty u(t)  dt leq K","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"$K > 0$: Total control budget\n$\\bar{u}$: Maximum intensity at any time","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"This approach generalizes older models that assume rigid time intervals for control.","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html#**Admissible-Control-Set**","page":"Introduction","title":"Admissible Control Set","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"We define the space of allowed controls:","category":"page"},{"location":"index.html","page":"Introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"mathcalU = u  0infty) to 0baru  u text is measurable and  u_L^1 leq K ","category":"page"},{"location":"constant_infection.html","page":"-","title":"-","text":"function SIRocp(tf, S0, I0, Q, β, γ, umax)\n\t    ocp = @def begin\n\t        t ∈ [0, tf], time\n\t        x = (S, I, C) ∈ R^3, state\n\t        u ∈ R, control\n\t\n\t        S(0) == S0\n\t        I(0) == I0\n\t        C(0) == Q\n\t\n\t        C(tf) ≥ 0.0\n\t\n\t        0 ≤ u(t) ≤ umax\n\t\n\t        0.0 ≤ I(t) ≤ 1.0\n\t        0.0 ≤ S(t) ≤ 1.0\n\t\n\t        ẋ(t) == [-(1 - u(t)) * β * S(t) * I(t),\n\t                  (1 - u(t)) * β * S(t) * I(t) - γ * I(t),\n\t                -u(t)]\n\t\n\t        -S(tf) → min\n\t    end\n\t\n\t    sol = solve(ocp, :direct, :adnlp, :ipopt; \n\t                    disc_method = :gauss_legendre_3, \n\t                    grid_size=1000, \n                            tol=1e-9, \n\t                    display=true)\n            return sol\nend","category":"page"}]
